From 923c6e576be1f9ba1fc9c2f8f21a7b995460d816 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 06:07:25 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] shared/tinyusb: USB callbacks run with the scheduler held.

A transfer callback run from a direct mp_usbd_task() call (the USB-CDC
stdin poll at the REPL, or a stdout write that waits) ran with the
scheduler unlocked, so a timer's scheduled callback could run between two
of its bytecodes. A program reading a usb.device CDC port from a timer
then read the port's receive buffer in the middle of the callback that
fills it, submitted a second OUT transfer, and the buffer's next
finish_write() failed its assert: the OUT endpoint never restarted and the
port went deaf. The scheduler is now held while TinyUSB runs, as it is when
the task runs from the scheduler.
---
 shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c b/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c
index 4a1de4079..1f6d0e1ff 100644
--- a/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c
+++ b/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c
@@ -537,7 +537,16 @@ void mp_usbd_task(void) {
 static void mp_usbd_task_inner(void) {
     in_usbd_task = true;
 
+    // Python's USB callbacks run inside tud_task_ext(). This task is also
+    // entered directly, from the USB-CDC stdin poll or a stdout write that
+    // waits, and then the scheduler isn't locked: a scheduled callback (a
+    // timer, say) could run between two bytecodes of a transfer callback and
+    // call into the same driver while its buffers are half updated. Hold the
+    // scheduler while TinyUSB runs, as it is held when this task is itself
+    // run by the scheduler.
+    mp_sched_lock();
     tud_task_ext(0, false);
+    mp_sched_unlock();
 
     mp_obj_usb_device_t *usbd = MP_OBJ_TO_PTR(MP_STATE_VM(usbd));
 
-- 
